    {
      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "The results suggest that only sociocultural factors and body fat dissatisfaction adhere to the socio-sports model of disordered eating in Brazilian male athletes. Therefore, muscularity dissatisfaction, training regime structures, competitiveness levels and fat percentages do not form the aetiology of disordered eating for this population."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "Because self-reporting instruments were used as evaluative tools, the results may not reflect the reality because the responses given were subjective. The study is also limited in that it applies a doubly indirect method (skinfolds) to evaluate fat percentages. Weight and body fat measurements can influence the survey responses about body image and disordered eating. However, it is noted that the questionnaires were not completed immediately after the anthropometric measurements, which minimised possible influences. Finally, the heterogeneity of the sample with respect to age and type of sport could have influenced the results."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "Further research on male athletes is encouraged to confirm the findings of this study (external validity). In addition, the construction of a socio-sports model for female athletes is recommended."
